---
import SlotStates from "../../../components/studies/SlotStates.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "ship-slot-language");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section aria-labelledby="purpose">
		<h2 id="purpose">01 / A mounting place that explains itself</h2>
		<p>
			A slot is a fixed mounting place on the ship: it has a position, an
			orientation and rules about what fits. The installed item supplies its
			appearance and function. An empty slot still needs to explain its
			possibilities. The starting visual intent is a translucent square over the
			hull, a clearly colored border and restrained technical markings, with the
			hull shading visible beneath it.
		</p>
		<h3>Slot states before visual examples</h3>
		<p>
			Empty or filled describes the contents; active describes the slot being
			edited. Here, “active” provisionally means selected in the builder, not an
			engine firing. This gives four primary states:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Empty:</strong> available mounting place, not selected.</li>
			<li><strong>Filled:</strong> installed item, not selected.</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Active + empty:</strong>
				selected destination for fitting an item.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Active + filled:</strong>
				selected item that can be replaced or removed where permitted.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			A linked secondary shares the ordinary slot states but cannot be selected
			independently. Editing its primary affects the pair. This relationship is
			not engine-specific: E describes compatibility only, and other slot types
			can also be linked. This clarification supersedes the earlier open
			question about selecting either partner.
		</p>
		<p>
			Type and exhaust direction describe the mount or item, rather than adding
			selection states. Hover and keyboard focus can later accompany these
			states. The four primary states now frame the first visual exploration;
			hull layout, equipment balance and new damage mechanics remain outside
			this cycle.
		</p>
		<p>
			The four primary states and the non-selectable linked-secondary role now
			guide visual exploration.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="next">
		<h2 id="next">02 / Slot language on the Fighter</h2>
		<h3>02.1 / Four states, one hull</h3>
		<p>
			The first controls coupled fill and border colors too tightly to explore
			useful alternatives. This revision keeps the four-state comparison and
			adds independent styling for each slot plus a linked engine pair. Choose a
			numbered slot, then change its colors, corner spacing, position and
			markings. Reset restores the starting appearance while preserving settled
			geometry.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Settled in 02.1:</strong>
			slot fill width 54 px, height 64 px, fill-to-border gap 4 px, border
			weight 1.5 px. These use the playground’s preview units and apply to all
			six slots, including selected states. Size, gap and border-weight controls
			are removed. Side slots retain their original production mounting angles
			(±26°); restoring these corrects the straight-slot rendering bug. Empty
			slots use acid lime at 35% opacity; filled slots use void-dark at 35%,
			regardless of selection or linking. The palette is limited to shared
			ivory, void-dark and acid lime. Fill and opacity controls and the
			conflicting dark/yellow preset are removed. Empty-slot borders are
			void-dark; filled-slot borders are acid lime. Dashed linked-slot borders
			follow the same rule. The connector between linked slots uses the same
			color as their borders. Border color is fixed and its control is removed.
			Empty slots show a large centered E (engines), W (weapons) or + (generic),
			in the border color at 70% opacity. Filled slots show their item without
			internal slot markings. Slot-number labels retain ivory text with a 35%
			void-dark backing for readability over the hull. Additional type text
			(Weapons, Generic or Engines) runs upward from the lower-left border
			corner in the border color, with no background. It accompanies empty and
			filled slots without replacing their numbers. Corner colors remain open.
		</p>
		<SlotStates client:load />
		<p>
			This fixture starts from production Fighter geometry and mount centers;
			offsets remain experimental; the dimensions above are settled for this
			example. It studies appearance at builder scale, not flight readability or
			fitting behavior. The filled deck examples use the same battery symbol to
			isolate selection. The linked secondary is not independently selectable;
			its dashed indication remains a visual proposal. The empty-slot type
			selector previews E/W/+ markings only; it does not change production
			fitting rules.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Accepted reference:</strong>
			02.1 after adding the vertical type text, 2026-10-31. The source is
			preserved as SlotStatesAccepted021.svelte; reset reproduces its baseline
			(acid-lime selection corners, 5 px corner gap, zero positional offsets).
			Temporary browser control values are not saved and are not claimed as
			separately captured decisions. The numbered labels and their backing
			remain alongside the vertical text. Acceptance covers the slot language,
			not the placeholder equipment artwork.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="readiness">
		<h2 id="readiness">03 / Builder overlays, equipped items in flight</h2>
		<p>
			<strong>Feasible with the current stack.</strong>
			The accepted slot language belongs only in the ship builder. Test flight
			and survival must render the hull and equipped items without empty-slot
			boxes, slot backgrounds, borders, selection corners, numbers, type labels
			or link connectors. This boundary is now implemented in the production
			renderer.
		</p>
		<h3>What already fits</h3>
		<p>
			ShipBuilder and the canonical /lab/builder already share the same
			component. buildMounts supplies stable mount keys, positions, angles and
			installed parts for all three chassis. Validated loadouts already support
			empty optional mounts and one mirrored side-engine value. Existing ivory,
			void-dark and acid-lime tokens provide the accepted palette. No
			dependency, simulation or storage migration is needed for a
			presentation-only delivery.
		</p>
		<h3>Adaptations identified before delivery</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Separate slots from items.</strong>
				build-renderer.ts currently draws a rectangle for every mount inside
				drawBuild; FlightDeck calls it too. A builder-only overlay must be
				separated from shared hull/item rendering. Some current item graphics
				borrow the slot rectangle as their body, so removing that rectangle
				blindly would leave incomplete items. Preserve actual item bodies,
				barrels, nozzles and plume origins.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Keep overlay geometry separate from physical mounts.</strong>
				The study uses 7 preview units per ship unit; the builder currently uses
				5.2. Preserve the accepted 54 × 64, 4 px gap and 1.5 px stroke in a
				common builder reference coordinate system. These values must not
				replace mount geometry used for collision, nozzle positions or flight
				forces. Hit targets must follow the displayed rotated overlays.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Represent links explicitly.</strong>
				Both side buttons are selectable today. The accepted secondary must be
				non-selectable and identify its primary for assistive technology. Model
				the relationship independently of E type; the existing shared
				side-engine value can continue to drive paired fitting. A derived typed
				view model can express this without changing saved builds.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Use production drawing and accessible controls.</strong>
				The reference is SVG; the production ship uses Canvas plus HTML buttons.
				Shared slot-style definitions should supply the accepted tokens and
				geometry to the builder and Lab, with keyboard focus distinct from
				selection. The reference keeps two selected examples for comparison; the
				real builder has one active primary.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<h3>Compatibility decision — approved</h3>
		<p>
			Engine (E) accepts engines; Weapon (W) accepts weapons; Equipment (Q)
			accepts weapons and utilities; Universal (+) accepts every part category.
			Side mounts are Engine, hull mounts Equipment, and aft mounts Universal.
			Weapon-only is supported by the contract without changing existing chassis
			layouts. Internal G remains the saved part category for guns. The left
			side is the primary; the right is its non-selectable linked secondary.
		</p>
		<h3>Evidence required for delivery</h3>
		<p>
			Check all four states, non-selectable linked secondary, paired
			replacement, removal, save/reload and keyboard focus in the real builder.
			Compare all three chassis against the accepted reference. Inspect test
			flight and survival to confirm only equipped items remain and their
			nozzles/plumes are unchanged. Automated checks can establish these
			boundaries; human review still assesses readability and fidelity. Prior
			narrow-screen verification timed out and is not a pass. Delivery checks
			are recorded in part 04.
		</p>
		<p class="invitation">
			The accepted design and approved four-type mapping are implemented
			locally. Existing fitting rules and saved builds are preserved; human
			review of the delivered appearance remains pending.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2>04 / Integration and delivery</h2>
		<p>
			The owner authorized implementation after approving the four-type mapping.
			The shared slot contract owns compatibility; buildMounts exposes slot type
			and link identity. The Canvas renderer accepts explicit builder-only slot
			colors and shares item rendering with flight. ShipBuilder and /lab/builder
			use that same renderer and accessible hit targets. Existing version-1
			loadouts, mount positions, flight forces and nozzle geometry are
			preserved.
		</p>
		<p>
			The accepted 02.1 source remains in SlotStatesAccepted021.svelte. The
			playground above now uses the approved Q/Equipment and +/Universal
			taxonomy. Production item casings remain existing artwork; builder item
			graphics are fitted inside the overlay while flight retains their existing
			scale. Full delivery validation and human review are recorded separately;
			source changes alone do not establish visual fidelity.
		</p>
		<p>
			Local verification: contracts, commit-history validation, lint, types,
			unit tests and production build passed. All 15 browser tests passed on a
			clean run. Backend rules and competition checks could not run because Java
			is unavailable. The unified verification result therefore remains failed;
			it is not a release certificate. Human review of the delivered builder
			remains pending. No push or deployment was performed.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
.invitation {
	border-left: 2px solid var(--color-acid-lime);
	padding-left: var(--spacing-md);
}
</style>
